<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lady Hyegyŏng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1735">1735</a>–<a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Queen Hŏngyŏng, Lady Hong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lady Hyegyŏng of the Pungsan Hong clan was a Korean writer and Crown Princess of the Joseon period, the wife of Crown Prince Sado and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">King Jeongjo</a>. Put forward for selection in 1743 at the age of nine, she married Sado in 1744 and lived through his worsening mental illness, during which he grew violent. In 1762 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeongjo-of-joseon">King Yeongjo</a> had Sado sentenced, and he was pronounced dead eight days later; she chose to stay alive to support her son and grandson. Between 1795 and 1806 she wrote The Memoirs of Lady Hyegyŏng (Hanjungnok, 'A Record of Sorrowful Days') in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a>, four narratives on her life, her family and her husband's illness and death.</p></div>
